   "text": "I read the drawing as a linear sequence of residues, which I wrote out in order and then closed into the macrocycle through the triazole.\n\n**Sequence, N-terminus to C-terminus**\nAsp \u2013 alkyne-bearing residue \u2013 Ala \u2013 Trp \u2013 His \u2013 Leu \u2013 Gly \u2013 Glu \u2013 Leu \u2013 Val \u2013 Trp \u2013 Lys(triazole) \u2013 Thr-NH2.\n\n- **Asp (N-terminus):** the N carries a wavy-bond stub that looks like an isopropyl fragment. I wrote it as an N-isopropyl group; this is the least certain part of the reading.\n- **Triazole staple:** the second residue has a CH2CH2 side chain ending in C4 of a 1,2,3-triazole. N1 of the triazole connects through four CH2 groups to the Lys alpha carbon, which closes the ring.\n- **Remaining residues:** Ala, Trp, His (imidazole with an NH), Leu, Gly, Glu (free CO2H), Leu, Val, Trp and Thr-NH2 are all drawn as plain peptide-linked residues.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- I worked out the CIP descriptors from the wedges and hashes at the triazole-bearing residue, Lys and Thr. All three come out S, matching L-amino acids.\n- Asp, Ala, Trp, His, Leu, Glu, Val and the second Trp have wedged bonds. I took these as L.\n- For Thr, the drawing shows the alpha carbon as S and the OH on the beta carbon as a wedge. I assigned the beta carbon as R, as in L-Thr.\n\nSMILES: CC(C)N[C@@H](CC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCc1cn2nn1)C(=O)N[C@@H](C)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1c[nH]c3ccccc13)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1c[nH]cn1)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)NCC(=O)N[C@@H](CCC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1c[nH]c3ccccc13)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCC2)C(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)O)C(N)=O"
